    if (!file) return;
    setIsLoading(true);
    try {
      await evaluateContract(file, instructions);
      setFile(null);
      setInstructions('');
    } catch {
      // error-display step will handle this
    } finally {
      setIsLoading(false);
    }
  }
```

```typescript
      <p className="sr-only" role="status">
        {isLoading ? 'Evaluating contract, please wait...' : ''}
      </p>

      {isLoading ? (
        <LoadingShimmer />
      ) : (
        <>
          <FileDropZone file={file} onFileChange={setFile} />
          <textarea ... />
        </>
      )}

      <button disabled={!file || isLoading} onClick={handleSubmit}>
        Evaluate Contract
      </button>
```

Restate in your own words: what happens on submit (happy path vs error), why is the state reset in `try` not `finally`, and what does the `role="status"` element do?

Good instinct but Suspense won't help here — React Suspense is for code-splitting and data fetching with frameworks that support it (like Next.js server components with `use()`). Our case is a client-side `fetch` triggered by a button click, which is imperative — Suspense needs a render-time data source.

What *would* clean it up is React 19's `useTransition` or `useActionState` — those are the "sexy new patterns":

```typescript
// useActionState (React 19) — manages pending + result in one hook
const [state, submitAction, isPending] = useActionState(
  async (_prev, formData) => {
    const file = formData.get('file') as File;
    return await evaluateContract(file, instructions);
  },
  null
);
// isPending replaces isLoading — no manual setState
// state holds the result — no separate result state needed
```

This eliminates `isLoading`, the `try/catch/finally`, and the manual state management. The `isPending` flag is managed by React itself.

Want me to try the `useActionState` approach? It would require wrapping in a `<form>` (which the review flagged as missing anyway), and we'd get Enter-key submission for free.

