content.text:
Close but one correction — we *are* catching the error. The `catch` block is there, it just doesn't do anything yet:

```typescript
    try {
      await evaluateContract(file, instructions);
      setFile(null);        // only on success
      setInstructions('');   // only on success
    } catch {
      // error-display step will handle this
    } finally {
      setIsLoading(false);  // always runs
    }
```

So on error the file and instructions are **preserved** (reset is in `try`, not `finally`), and the error is swallowed silently — it does *not* bubble to an error boundary. The user just sees the form come back with their file still staged. The error-display step will add user-visible feedback in that catch block.

Your point about the shimmer is right though — the accessibility is now self-contained inside `LoadingShimmer` with `role="status"` and the sr-only announcement.

| File | Level | Note |
|---|---|---|
| page.tsx | Medium | Understood the flow but missed that catch swallows the error (said it bubbles to error boundary) |

Follow-up (type **`skip`** to move on): what would happen if we removed that empty `catch` block entirely?

