Now **Understand** — walkthrough. Three new files this time. Snippets mode.

## File 1 of 3: `frontend/app/evaluate-contract/eval-result-context.tsx`

Client-side store for evaluation results. Uses a `Map` in a `useRef` to avoid re-renders on writes. The context value is memoized so consumers don't re-render unnecessarily.

```typescript
export function EvalResultProvider({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  const results = useRef(new Map<string, EvalResponse>());

  const value = useMemo<EvalResultContextValue>(() => ({
    setResult: (id, result) => results.current.set(id, result),
    getResult: (id) => results.current.get(id),
  }), []);

  return (
    <EvalResultContext value={value}>
      {children}
    </EvalResultContext>
  );
}
```

Restate: why `useRef` instead of `useState`, and why `useMemo` on the value object?

Exactly right. The Map is a side-channel — we write to it, then navigate away. No component needs to re-render when a result is stored.

## File 2 of 3: `frontend/app/evaluate-contract/page.tsx` (success path changes)

```typescript
async function evaluateContract(file: File, instructions: string): Promise<EvalSuccess> {
  const text = await file.text();
  const res = await fetch('/api/evaluate', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ text, instructions }),
  });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('evaluation failed');
  const data = await res.json();
  if (EvalErrorSchema.safeParse(data).success) throw new Error('eval error');
  const parsed = EvalSuccessSchema.safeParse(data);
  if (!parsed.success) throw new Error('unexpected response shape');
  return parsed.data;
}
